- Exception: `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DailyLimitExceededException.java` (checked exception, carries the limit).
- Handler (429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS, body `{"error": "..."}`): `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java` lines 14-16.
- Escalation wiring: `src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.DailyLimitExceededException.yml`.

---

## 4. Existing tests

The test suite is black-box acceptance-style over `POST /api/owners` + `GET /api/owners/{id}`. There are only three Java test files, all under `src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/`.

- `AcceptanceTest.java` lines 14-19 — the only active `@Test`, `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay()`. It creates an owner and asserts the returned `registrationDate` is never Saturday/Sunday. Important mismatch: the comment (lines 9-11) states the default registration date "must fall on a business day (a weekend rolls forward to Monday)", but `DefaultRegistrationDate.java` uses plain `LocalDate.now()` with no rollover — so this test fails on weekends.
- `AcceptanceBase.java` — the shared harness (`@SpringBootTest`, `@Transactional` rollback, `@WithMockUser` admin). Relevant helpers: `createOwnerOk` (lines 160-162), `ownerNode()` (117-125), `createMany(n)` (lines 183-187, "all dated today — used to drive the per-day create-limit / bulk-signup rules"), `fillCity` (170-179), `today()` (110-112). Its class javadoc (lines 36-38) explicitly names the "membership number" and "per-day cap" as count/quota rules the tests exercise.
- `AuditLogCapture.java` — audit-log test support (not directly registration/membership/limit).

There are **no dedicated unit tests** for `DefaultRegistrationDate`, `AssignMembershipNumber`, or `EnsureDailyLimit` individually; coverage is only the single acceptance test plus the base helpers set up to drive these rules. No test currently asserts the membership-number format or actually pushes past the 100/day limit.

